akes nasty remarks about colleagues. Strengths: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence (weather, computer). Weakness: interpersonal (nasty remarks) harms crew cooperation, memory for numbers could affect attention/accuracy. But overall maybe still strong.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Negatives: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education. Strengths: decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence. Weaknesses: crew cooperation, adaptability, learning.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Negatives: regarded as know-it-all, has a hot temper. Strengths: adaptability, attention, problem solving, responsibility. Weakness: crew cooperation (know-it-all, hot temper) could affect teamwork.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. All have some interpersonal issues. Which candidate has least severe? B has nasty remarks (bad). A is show-off, not open to new ideas (bad). C is egocentric, communication difficulty, not willing to further education (bad). D has hot temper, know-it-all (bad). All have issues. Need to weigh strengths.\n\nConsider stress resilience: B handles stress well, C keeps calm, D responds adequately, A not mentioned. So B and C strongest.\n\nTechnical competence: A sees complex connections, spatial vision; B good at weather, computer; C understands complicated tech; D solves problems extremely well. All decent.\n\nDecision quality: C makes correct decisions quickly; D solves problems extremely well; A anticipates dangerous situations; B not mentioned.\n\nCrew cooperation: A show-off, not open; B nasty remarks; C egocentric, communication difficulty; D hot temper, know-it-all. All have issues.\n\nResponsibility: D takes responsibility seriously; A very good leadership; B very conscientious; C not mentioned.\n\nWhich candidate has best balance? B has strong reliability (conscientious), stress handling, weather assessment (important for long-distance), computer skills (modern). Interpersonal issue is nasty remarks, but maybe can be addressed. A's not open to new ideas could affect adaptability. C's unwillingness to further education is a red flag for long-term development. D's hot temper could be dangerous under stress.\n\nThus B seems best overall despite interpersonal issue. Could argue that B's strengths outweigh. So I will recommend Candidate B.\n\nWe need to reference discussion (none) but can say \"Given the current lack of discussion, I propose...\". Use facts: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Also note negative: nasty remarks, weak memory for numbers. Explain why still best.\n\nProceed."}
